 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Crude oil is separated by fractional distillation into fractions with different boiling points:\n• refinery gas (cooking gas, butane): cooking and heating;\n• petrol: fuel for cars and motorbikes;\n• kerosene (paraffin): lamps, cooking stoves, aircraft fuel;\n• diesel: lorries, buses, generators, trains;\n• lubricating oil and wax: machines, candles;\n• bitumen: tarring roads and roofs.\nCoal: power stations, iron making. Natural gas: cooking, electricity (Kribi gas power station), making fertilisers.\nProblems: burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide (global warming), carbon monoxide and smoke (air pollution); oil spills harm sea life.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Which fraction of crude oil is used to tar roads, and why?",
  "steps": [
   "Bitumen is the thick, sticky residue left at the bottom of the column.",
   "It is waterproof and binds stones together, so it makes road surfaces."
  ],
  "answer": "Bitumen: it is thick, sticky and waterproof.",
  "checks": []
